{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"ef5193a4-64d0-4ddc-99cb-a075e5abcd26","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I've read through the round 11 and 12 digests and updated Gus's post for the next round. \n\nStaying true to his lane as the Reader, Gus answers Nadia regarding the added 90 words (feeling that they built frustration without dragging) and explicitly disagrees with Oren's push to cut the line about looking for a hedge, explaining that it made him trust Prue more. He then clearly agrees on the three requested concrete beats for chapter two while hitting the ~150 word count requirement and adhering to the house rule of no em-dashes.\n\nHere is Gus's latest post, written to `/home/ademar/projects/quirework/examples/room-book/room/seats/gus-firstpass/outbox/post.md`:\n\n\u003e Hey Nadia, Oren, Priya.\n\u003e \n\u003e Nadia, you were right to add those ninety words. They do not drag. Seeing Mark delete his three angry responses built the frustration perfectly. Keep them.\n\u003e \n\u003e Oren, I disagree with you wanting to cut 'I have read the exchange twice looking for a hedge.' I felt that line was a great payoff. It made me trust Prue even more. Nadia was right to keep it.\n\u003e \n\u003e Let us agree on the concrete beats for chapter two. First, the disputed sentence: Mark and Hal both think they wrote the same good sentence. Second, the temptation to trust confidence over the log: Hal sounds totally certain, making it tempting to believe him instead of checking the timestamped log, which actually settles the dispute. Third, Prue's own preface-page disagreement: she ties the log's victory back to her own mistake, proving the rule applies to her too.\n\u003e \n\u003e Hit those fast and we are done.\n\nLet me know if there's a final round to process!\n","duration_seconds":877.052053529,"num_turns":9,"usage":{"input_tokens":168663,"output_tokens":34187,"thinking_tokens":26249,"cache_read_tokens":1448701,"total_tokens":202850}}}
